Here are the candidates. Assess each one in this order and do not vary it.

[PASTE ALL CVs. Label each clearly: CANDIDATE 1: <name>, and so on.]

For each candidate:
1. TENURE LINE — every role with its start date, end date and duration in months or years, in chronological order. Flag concurrent roles and gaps explicitly. Where a CV gives a year without a month, assume mid-year and mark it as assumed.

2. KILL GATE — apply the kill criteria before anything else. State pass or fail on each, with the line of the CV that decides it. If the candidate fails, say so and continue to score them anyway, so the batch remains comparable.

3. SCORING — every driver, scored on the fixed scale, each with a V or I mark and one line of justification naming what in the CV produced the score.

4. WEIGHTED SCORE AND FLOOR — both, calculated, shown.

5. KILLS — bullets. Only real ones. Do not pad to a round number and do not soften.

6. STRENGTHS — bullets. Only real ones. A candidate you are rejecting still has real strengths and they belong on the page; a candidate you like does not get invented ones.

7. THREE QUESTIONS — the three questions to put to this specific candidate, aimed at this candidate's specific doubt. Generic interview questions are worthless here. For each, one short line on what it tests.

8. RECOMMENDATION — one line. If they are wrong for this seat but right for a different one, say which seat and say it plainly. Losing a good hire because they failed the wrong scorecard is a waste.

Standing instructions for this stage:
- Score what is evidenced, not what is claimed.
- If a portfolio, work sample or required attachment was not supplied, score it as absent. Do not assume it arrived separately — but say that you are assuming it did not, so I can correct you.
- Where a CV's self-description conflicts with its own employment history, name the conflict.
- Do not adjust the weights. Do not add or remove drivers.
